Here’s a minimal, self‑contained HTML page that shows the word **DVD** moving around the screen.  
The “logo” travels in straight lines, bounces off the window edges, and keeps going forever.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>DVD Logo Demo</title>
<style>
  body{margin:0;overflow:hidden;background:#000;color:#fff;font-family:sans-serif;}
  #dvd{
    position:absolute;
    font-size:48px;
    user-select:none;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="dvd">DVD</div>

<script>
const dvd = document.getElementById('dvd');
let x = 50, y = 50;          // starting position
let vx = 2, vy = 3;          // velocity (pixels per frame)

function move() {
  const w = window.innerWidth;
  const h = window.innerHeight;

  // Update position
  x += vx;
  y += vy;

  // Bounce on edges
  if (x <= 0 || x + dvd.offsetWidth >= w) vx *= -1;
  if (y <= 0 || y + dvd.offsetHeight >= h) vy *= -1;

  // Apply to element
  dvd.style.left = `${x}px`;
  dvd.style.top  = `${y}px`;

  requestAnimationFrame(move);
}

move();   // start animation
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**How it works**

- The `<div id="dvd">DVD</div>` is the placeholder logo.
- JavaScript updates its `left`/`top` style each frame.
- When the element reaches a window edge, the velocity component reverses (`* -1`) to create a bounce effect.

Save this as an `.html` file and open it in any browser. The text “DVD” will move smoothly across the screen, bouncing off all four walls.
